# Line endings.
#
# Many checks in this repo read files as text and search them for fixed
# strings, including strings that span lines. The shell scripts and the
# Rust, C# and Swift checks all do this. Evidence files also record SHA-256
# hashes of the committed bytes. Git for Windows turns LF into CRLF on
# checkout by default (core.autocrlf=true), which breaks both. So every text
# file is checked out with LF on every machine, Windows included.
#
# Windows tools cope with LF: Visual Studio, MSBuild, dotnet, PowerShell 5.1
# and 7, and Windows Notepad all read it. The one exception is a batch file,
# which can misbehave with LF, so *.bat and *.cmd are left exactly as
# committed (android/gradlew.bat is already CRLF).

* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh      text eol=lf
*.rs      text eol=lf
*.cs      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.xml     text eol=lf
*.csproj  text eol=lf
*.props   text eol=lf
*.targets text eol=lf
*.config  text eol=lf
*.xaml    text eol=lf
*.appxmanifest text eol=lf
*.manifest text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.sha256  text eol=lf
*.expected text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.swift   text eol=lf
*.kt      text eol=lf
*.kts     text eol=lf
*.ps1     text eol=lf
*.sln     text eol=lf

# Batch files: keep the committed bytes.
*.bat     -text
*.cmd     -text

# Binary files: never convert, never diff as text.
*.png     binary
*.ico     binary
*.jar     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.zip     binary
*.msix    binary
*.appx    binary
*.exe     binary
*.dll     binary
*.pfx     binary
*.cer     binary
